#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932 :topic/id #uuid "cd2e9db4-6727-5887-90bc-20eb0a0bf6d2" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059862 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059441 :question/id #uuid "e52f80d1-431d-51a0-9c51-950dc1a91065" :question/math-academy-id "e-138" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = 6x^3$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the constant multiple and power rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 3x^{2} \\\\\n&= 18x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffc1d505-17e4-5b0f-9150-506f9edb3350" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059442 :question/id #uuid "2385b6c3-1f86-59fe-96bf-d42ed5cd1b56" :question/math-academy-id "e-139" :question/problem "If $y=-\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "852449ca-b4df-5142-9540-bb503d5b7b62" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Negative Power Function Using the Constant Multiple Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059443 :question/id #uuid "72830b06-6333-5b61-8257-3967312bd178" :question/math-academy-id "e-140" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = x^4 -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} + x$." :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}} + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4} - x^{-2} + x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{4}) - \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} - (-2)x^{-3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + 2x^{-3} + 1 \\\\\n&= 4x^{3} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3469bdd-a201-5219-a734-ed3caa6b039a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions Without Constant Factors Using the Sum Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059865 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059444 :question/id #uuid "acfa9025-f984-5b09-9e24-f72707ad67ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-141" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ for $y= 2x^3 + 6\\sqrt x$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we convert the radical into a rational exponent, and we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3} + 6x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x^{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3}) + 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3x^{(3 - 1)} + 6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x^{(1/2 - 1)} \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + 3 \\cdot x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= 6x^{2} + \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :topic/math-academy-id 278 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :topic/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317 :topic/id #uuid "ebb1ef31-5cab-5892-affd-92e347cc4f35" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184340 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183955 :question/id #uuid "150ffc7c-4e70-5f9d-b8e2-8aceacfbd488" :question/math-academy-id "e-14883" :question/problem "Find $(f \\circ g)(x)$ for $f(x) = 3x^2 - 2x$ and $g(x) = x + 4$." :question/worked-solution "Note that $(f \\circ g)(x)$ is equivalent to $f(g(x))$.\n\nTo find $f(g(x))$, we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3x^{2} - 2x \\\\\nf(g(x)) &= 3(g(x))^{2} - 2(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}g(x)} = {\\color{red}x+4}\n$$\n\ninto the right-hand side of the above and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(g(x)) &= 3(g(x))^{2} - 2(g(x)) \\\\\n&= 3(x + 4)^{2} - 2(x + 4) \\\\\n&= 3(x + 4)(x + 4) - 2x - 8 \\\\\n&= 3(x^{2} + 4x + 4x + 16) - 2x - 8 \\\\\n&= 3(x^{2} + 8x + 16) - 2x - 8 \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} + 24x + 48 - 2x - 8 \\\\\n&= 3x^{2} + 22x + 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8569b3c6-28a0-5a45-902f-c513e6def877" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Composite Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184341 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183956 :question/id #uuid "64ae97f6-016a-5ebf-87a0-4731c2970c1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14886" :question/problem "If $g(x)=x^2+x$, then find $(g\\circ g)(x)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $(g\\circ g)(x)$ is equivalent to $g(g(x))$.\n\nTo find $g(g(x))$, we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= x^{2} + x \\\\\ng(g(x)) &= (g(x))^{2} + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}g(x)} = {\\color{red}x^2+x}\n$$\n\ninto the right-hand side of the above and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(g(x)) &= (g(x))^{2} + g(x) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + x)^{2} + (x^{2} + x) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + x)(x^{2} + x) + x^{2} + x \\\\\n&= (x^{4} + x^{3} + x^{3} + x^{2}) + x^{2} + x \\\\\n&= x^{4} + 2x^{3} + 2x^{2} + x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(g(x)) = x^4+2x^3+2x^2+x\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8092788d-bb38-5527-8069-8e32c97e08b9" :knowledge-point/title "Composing a Function With Itself"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184342 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183957 :question/id #uuid "95997b9c-6e35-5763-986a-74233b3dc707" :question/math-academy-id "e-14891" :question/problem "If $f(x) = x^2-1$, then find $f(a+1)$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f(a+1)$, we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $(a+1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{2} - 1 \\\\\nf(a + 1) &= (a + 1)^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(a + 1) &= (a + 1)^{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= (a + 1)(a + 1) - 1 \\\\\n&= a^{2} + a + a + 1 - 1 \\\\\n&= a^{2} + 2a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(a+1)=a^2+2a\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dd4252e-6408-5e03-828f-d3d94cccaa7c" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function With a Variable Input"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184343 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184044 :question/id #uuid "56db49ab-7850-5d9e-ab77-8aa05019521a" :question/math-academy-id "e-19768" :question/problem "If $f(x) = r + 4x$ and $f(5a+1) = 22a$, where $a$ and $r$ are constants, find an expression for $r$ in terms of $a$." :question/worked-solution "We are given the linear function\n\n$$\nf(x) = r + 4x\n$$\n\nand are told that\n\n$$\nf(5a+1) = 22a\n$$\n\nTo find $f(5a+1)$ in terms of $r$, we write down the function $f$ and replace every occurence of $x$ with $5a+1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= r + 4x \\\\\nf(5a + 1) &= r + 4(5a + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we simplify\n\n$$\nf(5a+1) = 22a\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(5a + 1) &= 22a \\\\\nr + 4(5a + 1) &= 22a \\\\\nr + 20a + 4 &= 22a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $r{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr + 20a + 4 &= 22a \\\\\nr &= 22a - 20a - 4 \\\\\nr &= 2a - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65538924-49b2-597e-a0c2-d4ff34112b2d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Involving Composite Function"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3817 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867}] :topic/title "Describing Function Composition"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644 :topic/difficulty 1.0054125078076268 :topic/id #uuid "5beaefa1-1d02-51a5-90bc-250adca28a57" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091951 :question/id #uuid "2f5afc63-6988-5676-b170-5826e659a5fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-144" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y = 2x^3 - 2x$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x) = 2x^3 - 2x.$ Computing the derivative $f'(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 6x^2-2.\n$$\n\nWe then compute $f'(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\nf'(1)  = 6(1)^2-2 = 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=4$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,0),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =4(x-1) \\\\ y & =4x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db808f48-6d0c-5b35-9342-b1817df65172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092383 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091952 :question/id #uuid "e248bb90-ba83-5e3f-938b-2c8be7d6eb7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-146" :question/problem "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=4\\sqrt{x}+4x$ at the point $(1,8)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4\\sqrt{x}+4x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\sqrt{x}+4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{1/2}+4x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = 2(1)^{-1/2}+4 = 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =6(x-1) \\\\ y & =6x-6+8 \\\\ y & =6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6x+2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $\\left(-\\dfrac 1 3, 0\\right).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c8ba29-95c9-5494-be0b-9b67bee6e45c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining where a Tangent Line Intersects an Axis"}] :topic/math-academy-id 986 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}]}
